==Review==
===Background===
{{See also|2022–23 Arsenal F.C. season}}

The 2022–23 campaign was a breakthrough season for Arsenal.<ref name="2022–23 season review TheAthletic">{{cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/4544231/2023/05/30/arsenal-season-review/ |title=Arsenal season review: Second-best, but a great team was forged |last=Campbell |first=Jordan |date=30 May 2023 |website=[[The Athletic]] |access-date=1 Jun 2023 |archive-date=30 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230530174233/https://theathletic.com/4544231/2023/05/30/arsenal-season-review/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Their primary goal at the start of the campaign was to qualify for next season's [[UEFA Champions League]].<ref name="2022–23 season review TheTelegraph">{{cite web |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2023/05/29/premier-league-2023-end-of-season-review/ |title=Who impressed most, and who must do better? Our experts' end-of-term Premier League reports |last1=Ducker |first1=James |last2=Dean |first2=Sam |last3=Percy |first3=John |last4=Law |first4=Matt |last5=Wilson |first5=Jeremy |last6=McGrath |first6=Mike |last7=Bascombe |first7=Chris |last8=Prentki |first8=Tom |date=29 May 2023 |website=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |access-date=1 Jun 2023 |archive-date=31 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230531052033/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2023/05/29/premier-league-2023-end-of-season-review/ |url-status=live}}</ref> At the halfway stage of the league season, the Gunners recorded their best-ever start to a Premier League campaign with 16 wins, two draws, one loss and 50 points from the first 19 games.<ref name="2022–23 season review TheAthletic"/> Arsenal led the league for a long time, but ultimately their challenge collapsed, as they collected just twelve points from their final nine matches (three wins, three draws and three losses).<ref name="2022–23 season review TheAthletic"/> Arteta's side finished second in the Premier League, returning to Champions League football for the first time since the 2016–17 season.<ref name="2022–23 season review TheTelegraph"/>

Arsenal were the second-youngest team in the [[2022–23 Premier League]] with an average starting age of 25 years and 52 days, eleven days older than relegated [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]], and two years and 201 days younger than champions [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/articles/ckvz53jp82po |title=Who had the youngest average starting XI? |date=31 May 2023 |website=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=1 Jun 2023 |archive-date=31 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230531215322/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/articles/ckvz53jp82po |url-status=live}}</ref> Four first-team players who were aged 24 or under on 1 July 2022 – [[Gabriel Magalhães]], [[Gabriel Martinelli]], [[Aaron Ramsdale]] and [[Bukayo Saka]] – signed new long-term contracts with the club during the campaign.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/football/bukayo-saka-new-contract-arsenal-fc-confirm-b1083082.html |title=Bukayo Saka: Arsenal confirm new contract as star becomes highest-paid player |last=Collings |first=Simon |date=23 May 2023 |website=[[Evening Standard]] |access-date=1 Jun 2023 |archive-date=6 Jun 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230606082434/https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/football/bukayo-saka-new-contract-arsenal-fc-confirm-b1083082.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Pre-season===
It was reported on 3 July 2023 that some first-team players who were not in action for their respective countries since the end of the previous campaign were back to the [[Arsenal Training Centre|London Colney training ground]] for pre-season training.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/first-day-back-london-colney-our-mens-team |title=First day back at London Colney for our men's team |date=3 Jul 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=4 Jul 2023 |archive-date=4 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230704020618/https://www.arsenal.com/news/first-day-back-london-colney-our-mens-team |url-status=live}}</ref>

Arsenal confirmed on 6 July that English forward and [[Arsenal F.C. Under-21s and Academy|academy]] graduate Reiss Nelson had signed a new long-term contract.<ref name="Nelson new contract AFC"/><ref name="Nelson new contract BBC"/> On the next day the Gunners announced that French defender William Saliba had penned a new long-term contract,<ref name="Saliba new contract BBC"/> and would wear the number 2 shirt from the 2023–24 season.<ref name="Saliba new contract AFC"/>

Two days later, Arteta's side travelled to Germany for a mini training camp at [[Adidas]] headquarters in [[Herzogenaurach]].<ref>{{cite tweet |author=Arsenal FC |user=Arsenal |number=1678097478191570946 |date=9 Jul 2023 |title=Next stop: Germany |access-date=10 Jul 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/pre-season-preparations-begin-germany |title=📸 Pre-season preparations begin in Germany |date=10 Jul 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=11 Jul 2023 |archive-date=11 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230711025212/https://www.arsenal.com/news/pre-season-preparations-begin-germany |url-status=live}}</ref> Three academy players – [[Myles Lewis-Skelly]], [[Ethan Nwaneri]] and [[Reuell Walters]] – were with the first-team squad.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/sport/football/news/mikel-arteta-promotes-three-arsenal-30436412 |title=Mikel Arteta promotes three Arsenal wonderkids to train with first team for pre-season |last=O'Brien |first=Josh |date=10 Jul 2023 |website=[[Daily Mirror]] |access-date=11 Jul 2023 |archive-date=11 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230711025207/https://www.mirror.co.uk/sport/football/news/mikel-arteta-promotes-three-arsenal-30436412 |url-status=live}}</ref> The Gunners faced [[2. Bundesliga]] side [[1. FC Nürnberg]] at [[Max-Morlock-Stadion]] in [[Nuremberg]] on 13 July. Saliba recovered from a back injury that had ruled him out of Arsenal's final eleven matches of the previous campaign to return to the starting line-up. Saka scored a goal on 7 minutes; but the Gunners were unable to double the lead, and an [[own goal]] from Jorginho in the second half ensured that the hosts claimed a 1–1 draw. The game also saw new signing and Germany international [[Kai Havertz]] make his non-competitive debut for Arsenal as a substitute at the break.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Jul-13/1-fc-nurnberg-1-1-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: FC Nurnberg 1–1 Arsenal |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=13 Jul 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=14 Jul 2023 |archive-date=13 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230713213109/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Jul-13/1-fc-nurnberg-1-1-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/fc-nurnberg-vs-arsenal/report/482486 |title=Nurnberg 1–1 Arsenal: Kai Havertz debuts as Gunners held in pre-season clash |date=14 Jul 2023 |website=[[Sky Sports]] |access-date=14 Jul 2023 |archive-date=14 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230714025857/https://www.skysports.com/football/fc-nurnberg-vs-arsenal/report/482486 |url-status=live}}</ref> They returned to England on the next day.

On 16 July, manager Arteta named a 29-man squad for the trip to the United States where they would play three more friendlies. The 18-year-old forward [[Amario Cozier-Duberry]] was the only academy player to travel with the first team to the US.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/us-tour-2023-travelling-squad |title=US Tour 2023: Arteta names 29-man squad |date=16 Jul 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=16 Jul 2023 |archive-date=16 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230716140239/https://www.arsenal.com/news/us-tour-2023-travelling-squad |url-status=live}}</ref>

Three days later, Arsenal faced [[Major League Soccer]]'s [[2023 MLS All-Star Game|All-Stars]] at [[Audi Field]] in [[Washington, D.C.|Washington]]. [[Gabriel Jesus]] and [[Leandro Trossard]] gave the Gunners a 2–0 lead at half-time. Jorginho scored a penalty after the restart. New signings [[Declan Rice]] and [[Jurriën Timber]] made their non-competitive debuts for the club on 65 minutes. Martinelli and Havertz added goals in the closing stages, making the score 5–0.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66253862 |title=Kai Havertz scores as Arsenal beat MLS All-Stars 5–0 |last=Stone |first=Simon |date=20 Jul 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=21 Jul 2023 |archive-date=20 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230720060639/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66253862 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="MLS v ARS Friendly AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Jul-20/mls-all-stars-0-5-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: MLS All-Stars 0–5 Arsenal |last=James |first=Josh |date=20 Jul 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=20 Jul 2023 |archive-date=20 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230720055505/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Jul-20/mls-all-stars-0-5-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

The Gunners took on Premier League side [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] at [[MetLife Stadium]] in [[East Rutherford, New Jersey|East Rutherford]], New Jersey on 22 July. [[Bruno Fernandes]] scored for United on 30 minutes with a long-range effort, although Arsenal goalkeeper Ramsdale could have done better. United's lead was doubled in the 37th minute through [[Jadon Sancho]]'s finish. The friendly also featured a post-match [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shootout]]. The Gunners lost that too after [[Fábio Vieira (footballer, born 2000)|Fábio Vieira]] skied his effort over the crossbar.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66280991 |title=Manchester United 2–0 Arsenal: Red Devils win New Jersey friendly |last=Stone |first=Simon |date=23 Jul 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=23 Jul 2023 |archive-date=24 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230724074113/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66280991 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Jul-22/arsenal-0-2-manchester-united-match-report |title=Report: Manchester United 2–0 Arsenal |last=James |first=Josh |date=22 Jul 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=23 Jul 2023 |archive-date=22 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230722235801/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Jul-22/arsenal-0-2-manchester-united-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 26 July, Arsenal played their third and final game of the 10-day USA tour against [[La Liga]] side [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] at [[SoFi Stadium]], the home of the [[National Football League]]'s [[Los Angeles Rams]] which is also owned by [[Kroenke Sports and Entertainment]]. Barcelona took the lead on 7 minutes through [[Robert Lewandowski]]. Saka levelled for the Gunners in the 13th minute, but he then sent a penalty wide. Barcelona reclaimed the lead through a deflected [[Raphinha]] free-kick in the 34th minute. Arteta's side drew level again on 43 minutes through Havertz. Trossard put the Gunners ahead in the 55th minute, then added his second on 78 minutes. [[Ferran Torres]] pulled one back for Barcelona on 88 minutes. Just a minute later, Vieira scored from 25 yards, helping the Gunners win 5–3.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66322660 |title=Arsenal 5–3 Barcelona: Leandro Trossard scores twice for Gunners in pre-season win |date=27 Jul 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=27 Jul 2023 |archive-date=27 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230727075118/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66322660 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Jul-27/arsenal-5-3-barcelona-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 5–3 Barcelona |last=James |first=Josh |date=27 Jul 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=27 Jul 2023 |archive-date=27 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230727054731/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Jul-27/arsenal-5-3-barcelona-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> Arteta's team flew back to London straight after the game.

On 28 July, Arsenal unveiled a bronze statue of [[List of Arsenal F.C. managers|former manager]] [[Arsène Wenger]] outside the North Bank Stand of [[Emirates Stadium]]. The statue, created by sculptor Jim Guy, is 3.5 metres (11 feet 6 inches) high, and depicts Wenger lifting the Premier League trophy. He joined five other Arsenal legends with statues outside the stadium: [[Tony Adams]], [[Dennis Bergkamp]], [[Herbert Chapman]], [[Ken Friar]] and [[Thierry Henry]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/arsene-wenger-statue-unveiled-emirates-stadium |title=Arsène Wenger statue unveiled at Emirates Stadium |date=28 Jul 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=29 Jul 2023 |archive-date=28 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230728225711/https://www.arsenal.com/news/arsene-wenger-statue-unveiled-emirates-stadium |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66341181 |title=Arsenal unveil Arsene Wenger bronze statue at Emirates Stadium |date=28 Jul 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=29 Jul 2023 |archive-date=29 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230729005548/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66341181 |url-status=live}}</ref>

The Gunners finished their pre-season campaign by beating [[Ligue 1]] side [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]] 5–4 on penalties following a 1–1 draw at home to claim the [[2023 Emirates Cup]] on 2 August. [[Youssouf Fofana (footballer, born 1999)|Youssouf Fofana]] scored for the visitors after the half-hour mark. Arsenal levelled in the 43rd minute through [[Eddie Nketiah]]. There were no further goals in the second half, and the game went to a penalty shootout. Gabriel Magalhães netted the winning spot-kick after Ramsdale saved [[Takumi Minamino]]'s effort.<ref name="ARS v ASM Friendly BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66389355 |title=Gabriel Jesus: Arsenal striker to miss start of Premier League season |date=2 Aug 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=3 Aug 2023 |archive-date=2 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230802231249/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66389355 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/emirates-cup-match-report-eddie-nketiah-gabriel-penalty-shootout |title=Report: Arsenal 1–1 Monaco (5-4 pens) |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=2 Aug 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=3 Aug 2023 |archive-date=3 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230803005130/https://www.arsenal.com/emirates-cup-match-report-eddie-nketiah-gabriel-penalty-shootout |url-status=live}}</ref> In the post-match press conference, Arteta confirmed that Gabriel Jesus had "some discomfort" in his right knee for the past few weeks and had undergone surgery to the knee. He would be out for a few weeks.<ref name="ARS v ASM Friendly BBC"/>

====First-team transfers (summer transfer window)====
The Premier League [[List of English football transfers summer 2023|summer transfer window]] ran from 14 June to 1 September 2023. The Gunners confirmed on 16 June that they were to release thirteen players,<ref name="Retained List"/> one of whom, [[Ainsley Maitland-Niles]], had made 132 appearances for Arsenal's first-team in all competitions.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/maitland-niles-its-been-emotional-journey |title=Maitland-Niles: "It's been an emotional journey" |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=16 Jun 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=16 Jun 2023 |archive-date=16 Jun 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230616124524/https://www.arsenal.com/news/maitland-niles-its-been-emotional-journey |url-status=live}}</ref>

