Arsenal announced their first signing of the summer on 28 June with 24-year-old Germany international [[Kai Havertz]] joining the club on a long-term contract from Premier League side [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]],<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/65984991 |title=Kai Havertz: Arsenal sign Chelsea forward in deal worth about £65m |date=28 Jun 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=29 Jun 2023 |archive-date=3 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230903122559/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/65984991 |url-status=live}}</ref> for a reported fee of [[Pound sterling|£]]62&nbsp;million plus £3&nbsp;million in add-ons.<ref name="Havertz TheAthletic"/> He would wear the number 29 shirt,<ref name="Havertz AFC"/> and would be the twelfth German to play for Arsenal's first team.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/13-things-you-may-not-know-about-kai-havertz |title=13 things you may not know about Kai Havertz |date=28 Jun 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=29 Jun 2023}}</ref>

On 30 June, the Gunners confirmed that Spanish defender [[Pablo Marí]], who had spent the entire previous season on loan with [[Serie A]] side [[AC Monza|Monza]], joined the Italian team on a permanent transfer,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66070704 |title=Pablo Mari: Arsenal defender joins AC Monza on a permanent deal |date=30 Jun 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=1 Jul 2023 |archive-date=1 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230701032940/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66070704 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Marí AFC"/> for a previously agreed fee reported to be [[Euro|€]]7&nbsp;million (£6&nbsp;million).<ref name="Marí Mirror"/>

It was announced on 6 July that Swiss midfielder and Arsenal's vice-captain [[Granit Xhaka]], who had made 297 appearances in all competitions and won two [[FA Cup]]s with the club, joined Bundesliga side Bayer Leverkusen on a permanent transfer for a reported fee of €25&nbsp;million (£21.4&nbsp;million).<ref name="Xhaka BBC"/><ref name="Xhaka AFC"/>

On 14 July, the Gunners confirmed that they had signed 22-year-old Dutch defender [[Jurriën Timber]] from [[Eredivisie]] side [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66017042 |title=Jurrien Timber: Arsenal sign Ajax & Netherlands defender for £34m |date=14 Jul 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=14 Jul 2023 |archive-date=14 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230714142041/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66017042 |url-status=live}}</ref> The transfer fee was reported to be worth an initial £34.3&nbsp;million (€40&nbsp;million), which could rise to £38.6&nbsp;million (€45&nbsp;million) with add-ons.<ref name="Timber TheAthletic"/> He was assigned the number 12 shirt,<ref name="Timber AFC"/> and would be the eighth Dutch player to feature for Arsenal's first team.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/12-things-you-may-not-know-about-jurrien-timber |title=12 things you may not know about Jurrien Timber |date=14 Jul 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=14 Jul 2023}}</ref> Timber has been an [[Arsenal F.C. supporters|Arsenal fan]] since he was a child.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/timber-ive-always-been-fan-arsenal |title=Timber: "I've always been a fan of Arsenal" |last=James |first=Josh |date=14 Jul 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=14 Jul 2023 |archive-date=14 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230714140641/https://www.arsenal.com/news/timber-ive-always-been-fan-arsenal |url-status=live}}</ref>

Arsenal announced on 15 July that 24-year-old English midfielder [[Declan Rice]], who was born and raised in London, had joined the club from Premier League side [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]] on a long-term contract,<ref name="Rice BBC"/><ref name="Rice TheAthletic">{{cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/4672030/2023/07/15/declan-rice-arsenal-transfer-complete/ |title=Arsenal sign Declan Rice from West Ham in £105m transfer |last=Ornstein |first=David |date=15 Jul 2023 |website=The Athletic |access-date=15 Jul 2023 |archive-date=15 Jul 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230715211808/https://theathletic.com/4672030/2023/07/15/declan-rice-arsenal-transfer-complete/ |url-status=live}}</ref> and would wear the number 41 shirt.<ref name="Rice AFC"/> It was reported that the transfer fee was an initial £100&nbsp;million plus £5&nbsp;million in add-ons,<ref name="Rice BBC"/><ref name="Rice TheAthletic"/> breaking Arsenal's [[List of Arsenal F.C. records and statistics#Transfers|transfer record fee]] paid for a player by £33&nbsp;million,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49197553 |title=Nicolas Pepe: Arsenal sign Lille winger for club record fee |date=1 Aug 2019 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=1 Jun 2023 |archive-date=15 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515110808/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49197553 |url-status=live}}</ref> and making him the [[List of most expensive association football transfers|most expensive English player]]<ref name="Rice TheAthletic"/> – the initial fee equalling the previous record held by [[Jack Grealish]].<ref name="Rice BBC"/>

The departures of three first-team players were announced afterwards. On 3 August, the Gunners confirmed that American defender [[Auston Trusty]] had joined Premier League side [[Sheffield United F.C.|Sheffield United]] on a permanent deal for a reported fee of £5&nbsp;million.<ref name="Trusty TheAthletic"/><ref name="Trusty AFC"/> Six days later, the club announced that American goalkeeper [[Matt Turner (soccer)|Matt Turner]] had joined Premier League side [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]] on a permanent transfer for a fee reported to be £10&nbsp;million.<ref name="Turner GOAL"/><ref name="Turner AFC"/> It was confirmed on 12 August that Brazilian forward [[Marquinhos (footballer, born 2003)|Marquinhos]] had joined Ligue 1 side [[FC Nantes|Nantes]] on a one-year loan.<ref name="Marquinhos AFC 1"/>

Arsenal confirmed on 15 August that the club had signed 27-year-old Spanish goalkeeper [[David Raya]] on a season-long loan from Premier League side [[Brentford F.C.|Brentford]] with the option to make the move permanent in summer 2024.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66512991 |title=David Raya: Arsenal sign Spain goalkeeper on season-long loan from Brentford |date=15 Aug 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=15 Aug 2023 |archive-date=15 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230815133258/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66512991 |url-status=live}}</ref> The initial loan fee was reported to be £3&nbsp;million, and activating the option would be for a further £27&nbsp;million.<ref name="Raya TheAthletic"/> He would take the number 22 shirt,<ref name="Raya AFC"/> and would be the fifteenth Spanish player to represent Arsenal's first team.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/quiz-name-every-spaniard-play-arsenal |title=Quiz: Name every Spaniard to play for Arsenal |last=James |first=Josh |date=4 Jul 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=4 Jul 2024 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240704165229/https://www.arsenal.com/news/quiz-name-every-spaniard-play-arsenal |archive-date=4 Jul 2024}}</ref>

The Gunners continued their summer exodus after signing four players. On 18 August, the club announced the loan departure of Icelandic goalkeeper [[Rúnar Alex Rúnarsson]] to Championship side [[Cardiff City F.C.|Cardiff City]].<ref name="Rúnarsson AFC 1"/> Nine days later, Arsenal confirmed that Scottish defender [[Kieran Tierney]] had joined La Liga side [[Real Sociedad]] on loan for the 2023–24 season;<ref name="Tierney AFC"/> the loan fee was reported to be around £1.2&nbsp;million (€1.4&nbsp;million).<ref name="Tierney football.london"/> The departure of American striker and [[Arsenal F.C. Under-21s and Academy|Arsenal academy]] graduate [[Folarin Balogun]] to Ligue 1 side [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]] was announced on 30 August.<ref name="Balogun TheAthletic"/><ref name="Balogun AFC"/> It was reported that the transfer fee was an initial €30&nbsp;million (£25.8&nbsp;million) plus €10&nbsp;million (£8.6&nbsp;million) in add-ons, and the terms of the deal included a 17.5% sell-on clause for Arsenal.<ref name="Balogun TheAthletic"/>

On English transfer deadline day, 1 September, four first-team players departed the club. Portuguese defender [[Nuno Tavares]] joined Premier League side Nottingham Forest on a season-long loan with the option to make the move permanent in summer 2024;<ref name="Tavares AFC"/><ref name="Tavares football.london"/> the initial loan fee was reported to be £1&nbsp;million, and activating the option would be for a further £12&nbsp;million.<ref name="Tavares football.london"/> English goalkeeper and academy graduate [[Arthur Okonkwo]] joined [[EFL League Two|League Two]] side [[Wrexham A.F.C.|Wrexham]] on loan for the 2023–24 season.<ref name="Arthur Okonkwo AFC"/> English defender [[Rob Holding]], who had made 162 first-team appearances in all competitions and won two FA Cups with the club, joined Premier League side [[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]] on a permanent transfer,<ref name="Holding AFC"/> for a reported fee of £1&nbsp;million with £2.5&nbsp;million in add-ons.<ref name="Holding TheAthletic"/> Belgian midfielder [[Albert Sambi Lokonga]] joined Premier League side [[Luton Town F.C.|Luton Town]] on a one-year loan.<ref name="Sambi Lokonga AFC"/>

On 9 September, Arsenal confirmed that Ivory Coast forward [[Nicolas Pépé]] had departed the club following the termination of his contract.<ref name="Pépé AFC"/>

After the summer transfer window closed, there were twenty-five players in the first-team squad: three goalkeepers, eight defenders, eight midfielders, and six forwards. Six other first-team players were out on loan.<ref name="first-team squad (Sep) AFC"/>

===August===
