Arsenal kicked off their season by taking part in the [[2023 FA Community Shield|FA Community Shield]] for the 24th time in their history, against [[Treble (association football)|Treble-winners]] Manchester City at [[Wembley Stadium]] on 6 August. New signings Havertz, Rice and Timber made their competitive debuts for the Gunners. City's [[Cole Palmer]] broke the deadlock on 77 minutes, curling a shot into the top corner. Trossard equalised for Arsenal in stoppage time when his shot deflected into the back of the net. The game ended 1–1 in normal time and went straight to a penalty shootout. The Gunners prevailed 4–1 from the spot with Vieira scoring the winning penalty, after [[Kevin De Bruyne]]'s drive hit the crossbar and [[Rodri (footballer, born 1996)|Rodri]]'s attempt was saved by Ramsdale.<ref name="ARS v MCI CS BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66352143 |title=Arsenal 1–1 Manchester City (4–1 on pens): Gunners win shootout to secure Community Shield |last=Rose |first=Gary |date=6 Aug 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=7 Aug 2023 |archive-date=20 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230920200746/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66352143 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="ARS v MCI CS AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fa-community-shield-match-report-trossard-penalty-shootout |title=Report: Arsenal 1–1 Man City (4–1 pens) |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=6 Aug 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=7 Aug 2023 |archive-date=6 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230806224658/https://www.arsenal.com/fa-community-shield-match-report-trossard-penalty-shootout |url-status=live}}</ref> This was Arsenal's 17th Community Shield win, making them the [[List of FA Community Shield matches|second-most successful club]] in the competition.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/arsenal-vs-manchester-city/report/482389 |title=Arsenal 1–1 Man City (4–1 on pens): Gunners lift Community Shield after shootout following Leandro Trossard's late equaliser |last=Smith |first=Peter |date=6 Aug 2023 |website=Sky Sports |access-date=7 Aug 2023 |archive-date=11 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230811003444/https://www.skysports.com/football/arsenal-vs-manchester-city/report/482389 |url-status=live}}</ref> The result meant that Arteta's side ended an eight-match losing run in all competitions against [[Pep Guardiola]]'s Manchester City, beating them for the first time since July 2020.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3618830 |title=Arsenal beat Man City on penalties to win Community Shield |date=6 Aug 2023 |website=Premier League |access-date=7 Aug 2023 |archive-date=6 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230806234546/https://www.premierleague.com/news/3618830 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Six days later, the Gunners played their first home game of the season against [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]]. Arteta's side took a 2–0 half-time lead through a Nketiah finish and a Saka strike. [[Taiwo Awoniyi]] pulled a late goal back for the visitors from a counter-attack.<ref name="ARS v NFO PL BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66413654 |title=Arsenal 2–1 Nottingham Forest: Superb Bukayo Saka strike helps Gunners to opening win |last=Salley |first=Emily |date=12 Aug 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=13 Aug 2023 |archive-date=13 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230813013445/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66413654 |url-status=live}}</ref> The game also saw Premier League debutant Timber limp off the pitch with an injury on 50 minutes.<ref name="ARS v NFO PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Aug-12/arsenal-2-1-nottingham-forest-fc-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 2–1 Nottingham Forest |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=12 Aug 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=13 Aug 2023 |archive-date=12 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230812152946/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Aug-12/arsenal-2-1-nottingham-forest-fc-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> Arsenal confirmed on 16 August that Timber had sustained an injury to the [[anterior cruciate ligament]] in his right knee and was set for surgery. The club did not give a timeframe for his absence.<ref name="Timber suffered ACL injury BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66524799 |title=Jurrien Timber: Arsenal defender suffers ACL injury and will have surgery |date=16 Aug 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=16 Aug 2023 |archive-date=16 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230816142629/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66524799 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Timber suffered ACL injury AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/jurrien-timber-medical-update |title=Jurrien Timber medical update |date=16 Aug 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=16 Aug 2023 |archive-date=16 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230816154253/https://www.arsenal.com/news/jurrien-timber-medical-update |url-status=live}}</ref>

Arsenal faced [[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]] at [[Selhurst Park]] on 21 August. Before the game, the team paid tribute to the injured Timber, as skipper Ødegaard held up a shirt for the team photo, which read "J. Timber 12" on the back.<ref name="CRY v ARS PL AFC 2">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/40-photos-resolute-win-over-crystal-palace |title=40 photos from our resolute win over Palace |last=Holbrook |first=Emma |date=22 Aug 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=22 Aug 2023 |archive-date=22 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230822155536/https://www.arsenal.com/news/40-photos-resolute-win-over-crystal-palace |url-status=live}}</ref> Ødegaard stroked home a spot-kick in the 53rd minute. Tomiyasu was controversially sent off by referee [[David Coote (referee)|David Coote]] on 67 minutes for two quick bookable offences – the first yellow was for delaying a throw-in, and the second was harshly awarded for a minor foul on Palace's [[Jordan Ayew]] in midfield. Despite playing the final half-hour with ten men, Arteta's side claimed a hard-fought 1–0 victory.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66495070 |title=Crystal Palace 0–1 Arsenal: Martin Odegaard scores only goal in Gunners win |last=Begley |first=Emlyn |date=21 Aug 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=22 Aug 2023 |archive-date=22 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230822014850/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66495070 |url-status=live}}</ref> The win clocked up the Gunners' 200th Premier League away clean sheet, making them the third side to do so.<ref name="CRY v ARS PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Aug-21/crystal-palace-0-1-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Crystal Palace 0–1 Arsenal |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=21 Aug 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=22 Aug 2023 |archive-date=22 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230822155536/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Aug-21/crystal-palace-0-1-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 26 August, Arteta's team played at home against [[Fulham F.C.|Fulham]]. Gabriel Jesus recovered from his knee injury to be named in a matchday squad for the first time; and Saka made his 83rd consecutive Premier League appearance for the Gunners, breaking the club record in the competition set by [[Paul Merson]] between 1995 and 1997.<ref name="ARS v FUL PL SkySports">{{cite web |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/arsenal-vs-fulham/report/482611 |title=Arsenal 2–2 Fulham: Joao Palhinha scores late equaliser as hosts' perfect start to Premier League season ended |last=Shread |first=Joe |date=26 Aug 2023 |website=Sky Sports |access-date=27 Aug 2023 |archive-date=26 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230826174015/https://www.skysports.com/football/arsenal-vs-fulham/report/482611 |url-status=live}}</ref> Arteta's side fell behind after just 57 seconds, as [[Andreas Pereira]] raced onto Saka's loose pass and fired in a curling shot from 25 yards,<ref name="ARS v FUL PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Aug-26/arsenal-2-2-fulham-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 2–2 Fulham |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=26 Aug 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=27 Aug 2023 |archive-date=26 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230826185730/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Aug-26/arsenal-2-2-fulham-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> with goalkeeper Ramsdale out of position.<ref name="ARS v FUL PL BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66552613 |title=Arsenal 2–2 Fulham: Joao Palhinha earns draw for 10-man Fulham away to Gunners |last=Thomas |first=Marissa |date=26 Aug 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=27 Aug 2023 |archive-date=26 Aug 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230826162740/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66552613 |url-status=live}}</ref> This meant that Arsenal had conceded inside the first minute in three of their last nine Premier League games at home (vs [[AFC Bournemouth|Bournemouth]], [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] and Fulham), becoming the first team in Premier League history to concede a first-minute goal three times in a single calendar year.<ref name="ARS v FUL PL SkySports"/> Saka atoned for his earlier error by scoring a penalty on 70 minutes. Moments later, substitute Nketiah tucked home a cross to put the hosts ahead.<ref name="ARS v FUL PL AFC"/> After blocking off Nketiah on a counter, [[Calvin Bassey]] was sent off for a second bookable offence in the 83rd minute.<ref name="ARS v FUL PL SkySports"/> Fulham's [[João Palhinha]] scored the equaliser from a corner on 87 minutes, making the score 2–2.<ref name="ARS v FUL PL AFC"/>

===September===
Ahead of the first [[FIFA International Match Calendar|international break]] of the season, Arsenal played at home against [[Arsenal F.C.–Manchester United F.C. rivalry|rivals]] Manchester United on 3 September. The visitors took the lead on 27 minutes as [[Marcus Rashford]] cut inside to beat Ramsdale at his far post with a curling strike.<ref name="ARS v MUN PL BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66631567 |title=Arsenal 3–1 Manchester United: Declan Rice and Gabriel Jesus injury-time goals stun Red Devils |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=3 Sep 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=4 Sep 2023 |archive-date=24 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230924161900/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66631567 |url-status=live}}</ref> Rashford's goal meant that Arsenal had conceded from the first shot they had faced in seven Premier League matches in 2023 – the most of any team in this period.<ref name="ARS v MUN PL SkySports">{{cite web |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/arsenal-vs-manchester-united/report/482621 |title=Arsenal 3–1 Man Utd: Declan Rice and Gabriel Jesus score in stoppage time to grab memorable win for Gunners |last=Smith |first=Peter |date=3 Sep 2023 |website=Sky Sports |access-date=4 Sep 2023 |archive-date=3 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230903232055/https://www.skysports.com/football/arsenal-vs-manchester-united/report/482621 |url-status=live}}</ref> Arteta's side responded in the 28th minute with a goal by Ødegaard.<ref name="ARS v MUN PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/report-arsenal-3-1-manchester-united |title=Report: Arsenal 3–1 Manchester United |last=James |first=Josh |date=3 Sep 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=4 Sep 2023 |archive-date=3 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230903192705/https://www.arsenal.com/news/report-arsenal-3-1-manchester-united |url-status=live}}</ref> In the 59th minute, the Gunners were awarded a penalty, but the decision was overturned after referee [[Anthony Taylor (referee)|Anthony Taylor]] checked the pitchside monitor.<ref name="ARS v MUN PL BBC"/> The visitors thought they had retaken the lead on 88 minutes when [[Alejandro Garnacho]] finished off a swift counter-attack, but a [[Video assistant referee|VAR]] check showed Gabriel Magalhães' sudden halting of his run was enough to catch Garnacho offside.<ref name="ARS v MUN PL TheAthletic">{{cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/4829185/2023/09/03/arsenal-manchester-united-premier-league-analysis/ |title=Arsenal 3 Manchester United 1: Rice delivers, VAR controversy, Hojlund's lively cameo |last1=Anka |first1=Carl |last2=de Roché |first2=Art |last3=Carey |first3=Mark |date=3 Sep 2023 |website=The Athletic |access-date=4 Sep 2023 |archive-date=4 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230904105120/https://theathletic.com/4829185/2023/09/03/arsenal-manchester-united-premier-league-analysis/ |url-status=live}}</ref> In the sixth minute of stoppage time, Rice controlled a Saka corner at the far post and slammed home his first Arsenal goal to send Emirates wild.<ref name="ARS v MUN PL TheAthletic"/> This goal on 95 minutes and 43 seconds was the latest winning goal ever scored in a Premier League fixture between the two rivals.<ref name="ARS v MUN PL SkySports"/> Five minutes later, Vieira sprung forward on the counter, fed Gabriel Jesus and the Brazilian ran through on goal, cut away from a sliding [[Diogo Dalot]] and slotted the ball past goalkeeper [[André Onana]], sealing a 3–1 victory.<ref name="ARS v MUN PL AFC"/> This was the first time the Gunners had won three consecutive home league games against the Red Devils since May 1991.<ref name="ARS v MUN PL SkySports"/> In the post-match presser, Arteta confirmed that [[Thomas Partey]] had picked up an injury in training before the game and would be out for weeks.<ref name="Partey suffered injury in Aug AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/arteta-gives-partey-update |title=Arteta gives Partey update |date=3 Sep 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=4 Sep 2023 |archive-date=4 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230904073648/https://www.arsenal.com/news/arteta-gives-partey-update |url-status=live}}</ref>

Sixteen Arsenal first-team players (excluding players who were loaned out) were named in their respective countries' senior squads for international fixtures in September: Trossard (Belgium), Gabriel Jesus, Gabriel Magalhães and Martinelli (Brazil), Nketiah, Ramsdale, Rice and Saka (England), [[Karl Hein (footballer)|Karl Hein]] (Estonia), Saliba (France), Havertz (Germany), Tomiyasu (Japan), Ødegaard (as Norway's captain), [[Jakub Kiwior]] (Poland), Raya (Spain), and [[Oleksandr Zinchenko (footballer)|Oleksandr Zinchenko]] (Ukraine).<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/all-16-players-called-international-duty |title=All 16 players called up for international duty |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=4 Sep 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=5 Sep 2023 |archive-date=5 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230905054829/https://www.arsenal.com/news/all-16-players-called-international-duty |url-status=live}}</ref> This was the first time for Nketiah to be called up to the England senior team.<ref name="England call-ups Aug 2023"/> Gabriel Magalhães made his senior debut for Brazil on 8 September 2023.<ref name="Magalhães senior intl debut AFC"/>

Following the season's first international break, the Gunners faced 18th-placed [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] away at [[Goodison Park]] on 17 September. Debutant [[David Raya]] started in goal and kept his first clean sheet for Arsenal in a 1–0 win. In the 69th minute, Trossard fired a first-time finish in off the post following a short corner. The result saw the Gunners' five-game winless run at Goodison Park in the Premier League come to an end.<ref name="EVE v ARS PL">{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3688364 |title=Super sub Trossard gives Arsenal first win at Everton since 2017 |date=17 Sep 2023 |website=Premier League |access-date=18 Sep 2023}}</ref><ref name="EVE v ARS PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Sep-17/everton-0-1-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Everton 0–1 Arsenal |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=17 Sep 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=18 Sep 2023 |archive-date=19 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230919014117/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Sep-17/everton-0-1-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

