After a six-year absence, Arsenal started their 20th Champions League campaign since the [[1992–93 UEFA Champions League|1992–93 season]] as they played at home on 20 September in their opening Group B match against the previous season's Eredivisie runners-up [[PSV Eindhoven]].<ref name="ARS v PSV UCL BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66857208 |title=Arsenal v PSV Eindhoven: Gunners mark Champions League return with ruthless win |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=20 Sep 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=21 Sep 2023 |archive-date=21 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230921005052/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66857208 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="ARS v PSV UCL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Sep-20/arsenal-4-0-psv-eindhoven-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 4–0 PSV Eindhoven |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=20 Sep 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=21 Sep 2023 |archive-date=24 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230924082914/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Sep-20/arsenal-4-0-psv-eindhoven-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Mohamed Elneny]], who was the only player left from the Gunners' [[2016–17 UEFA Champions League|2016–17 Champions League]] squad,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fotmob.com/news/1cebzhikghgy81mjg6h3at4l5p-arsenal-return-champions-league-bang |title=Arsenal return to Champions League with a bang |date=20 Sep 2023 |website=FotMob |access-date=21 Sep 2023 |archive-date=28 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230928225245/https://www.fotmob.com/news/1cebzhikghgy81mjg6h3at4l5p-arsenal-return-champions-league-bang |url-status=live}}</ref> was back on the bench for the first time since a serious knee injury in January 2023.<ref name="Elneny underwent surgery BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64470830 |title=Mohamed Elneny: Arsenal midfielder out for 'extended period' after knee surgery |date=31 Jan 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=1 Jul 2023 |archive-date=31 Jan 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230131144931/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64470830 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="ARS v PSV UCL line-up AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/team-news-mikel-makes-three-changes-ahead-psv |title=Team news: Mikel makes three changes ahead of PSV |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=20 Sep 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=21 Sep 2023 |archive-date=28 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230928225248/https://www.arsenal.com/news/team-news-mikel-makes-three-changes-ahead-psv |url-status=live}}</ref> Six of Arsenal's starting eleven – Raya, Rice, Saka, Saliba, Trossard and White – made their debuts in the competition on a rain-soaked night.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://hayters.com/mfv/five-things-we-learned-from-arsenal-4-0-psv/ |title=Five things we learned from Arsenal 4–0 PSV |last=Denis |first=James St |date=20 Sep 2023 |website=hayters.com |access-date=21 Sep 2023 |archive-date=28 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230928225248/https://hayters.com/mfv/five-things-we-learned-from-arsenal-4-0-psv/ |url-status=live}}</ref> The hosts took the lead on 8 minutes through Saka tucking in the rebound from Ødegaard's shot. In the 20th minute, Saka crossed for Trossard to blast the ball into the bottom corner. Eighteen minutes later, Trossard crossed for Gabriel Jesus at the back post to fire a shot into the net. Ødegaard rounded off the scoring in the 70th minute with a 20-yard strike. The 4–0 victory marked Arteta's first Champions League game in charge.<ref name="ARS v PSV UCL BBC"/><ref name="ARS v PSV UCL AFC"/>

On 22 September, the club announced that captain Ødegaard had signed a new long-term contract.<ref name="Ødegaard new contract AFC"/><ref name="Ødegaard new contract BBC"/> Two days later, Arsenal faced local rivals [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] at home in the first [[North London derby]] of the season. Already missing Timber to a long-term knee problem, the Gunners were also without Partey and had lost Martinelli and Trossard to injuries in a week before the game.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/4894403/2023/09/25/arsenal-tottenham-declan-rice/ |title=Without Declan Rice, Arsenal's lack of physicality in midfield hurt them against Spurs |last=de Roché |first=Art |date=25 Sep 2023 |website=The Athletic |access-date=25 Sep 2023 |archive-date=27 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230927161803/https://theathletic.com/4894403/2023/09/25/arsenal-tottenham-declan-rice/ |url-status=live}}</ref> In the 26th minute, Saka's shot was deflected into his own goal by Spurs defender [[Cristian Romero (Argentine footballer)|Cristian Romero]]. The visitors levelled through [[Son Heung-min]] in the 42nd minute. It continued a run of early-season injuries for Arteta's side, as Rice was forced off with a back issue and was replaced by Jorginho at half-time. Arsenal retook the lead on 54 minutes through a Saka penalty. Tottenham drew level again in the 55th minute as Jorginho was caught in possession by [[James Maddison]], who slipped a pass into Son to finish past Raya. The game ended 2–2.<ref name="ARS v TOT PL SkySports">{{cite web |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11661/12965754/arsenal-2-2-tottenham-bukayo-saka-and-heung-min-son-star-as-north-london-derby-ends-in-draw |title=Arsenal 2–2 Tottenham: Bukayo Saka and Heung-Min Son star as North London derby ends in draw |last=Blitz |first=Sam |date=24 Sep 2023 |website=Sky Sports |access-date=25 Sep 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Sep-24/arsenal-2-2-tottenham-hotspur-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 2–2 Tottenham Hotspur |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=24 Sep 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=25 Sep 2023 |archive-date=27 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230927161804/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Sep-24/arsenal-2-2-tottenham-hotspur-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 27 September, Arsenal entered the [[2023–24 EFL Cup|EFL Cup]] in the third round, facing Premier League side [[Brentford F.C.|Brentford]] away at [[Brentford Community Stadium]]. Jorginho started as the Gunners' captain for the first time; [[Emile Smith Rowe]] made his 100th senior appearance for Arsenal, and his first start for the club in 16 months; while Academy forward [[Charles Sagoe Jr]] was handed a first-team debut.<ref name="BRE v ARS EFLCup SkySports">{{cite web |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/brentford-vs-arsenal/report/498864 |title=Brentford 0–1 Arsenal: Reiss Nelson's early strike secures Gunners' spot in Carabao Cup fourth round |last=Wright |first=Nick |date=27 Sep 2023 |website=Sky Sports |access-date=28 Sep 2023 |archive-date=3 Oct 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231003215354/https://www.skysports.com/football/brentford-vs-arsenal/report/498864 |url-status=live}}</ref> Nelson netted the only goal of the game to clinch victory for the Gunners. The 1–0 result advanced them to the next round.<ref name="BRE v ARS EFLCup BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66862355 |title=Brentford 0–1 Arsenal: Aaron Ramsdale impresses on return as Gunners progress in EFL Cup |date=27 Sep 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=28 Sep 2023 |archive-date=27 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230927211757/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66862355 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="BRE v ARS EFLCup AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Sep-27/brentford-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Brentford 0–1 Arsenal |last=Wright |first=Stephen |date=27 Sep 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=28 Sep 2023 |archive-date=28 Sep 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230928021114/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Sep-27/brentford-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

Arteta's side headed to the south coast of England on 30 September, facing 17th-placed Bournemouth at [[Dean Court|Vitality Stadium]]. Arsenal took a 2–0 half-time lead through a Saka header and an Ødegaard penalty. The Gunners got another spot-kick early into the second half. This time Havertz was handed the ball by regular taker Saka to strike his first Arsenal goal on 53 minutes. White headed home from Ødegaard's free-kick in stoppage time, making it 4–0. This was the fourth time the Gunners had won their opening three away games in a top-flight campaign, and was the first time they had done so without conceding a single goal in their history.<ref name="BOU v ARS PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Sep-30/bournemouth-0-4-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Bournemouth 0–4 Arsenal |last=James |first=Josh |date=30 Sep 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=1 Oct 2023 |archive-date=1 Oct 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231001081931/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Sep-30/bournemouth-0-4-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> The win moved Arsenal to within one point of leaders Manchester City.<ref name="BOU v ARS PL BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66899190 |title=Bournemouth 0–4 Arsenal: Kai Havertz scores his first Gunners goal from penalty spot |last=Thomas |first=Marissa |date=30 Sep 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=1 Oct 2023 |archive-date=24 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240524195746/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/66899190 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===October===
Arsenal suffered their first defeat of the season with a 2–1 loss to Ligue 1 side [[RC Lens|Lens]] at [[Stade Bollaert-Delelis]] in the Champions League on Tuesday, 3 October, after being late arriving to northern France on Monday night with their flight delayed by around five hours due to bad weather.<ref name="LEN v ARS UCL SkySports">{{cite web |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/rc-lens-vs-arsenal/report/498899 |title=Lens 2–1 Arsenal: Mikel Arteta's side beaten for first time this season as Bukayo Saka limps off injured |last=Blitz |first=Sam |date=3 Oct 2023 |website=Sky Sports |access-date=4 Oct 2023 |archive-date=4 Oct 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231004115457/https://www.skysports.com/football/rc-lens-vs-arsenal/report/498899 |url-status=live}}</ref> In the 14th minute, Saka seized upon [[Adrien Thomasson]]'s loose pass and quickly set up Gabriel Jesus to rifle home and put the Gunners ahead. Eleven minutes later, Raya's pass to Tomiyasu was intercepted, and the ball eventually went to Thomasson, who unleashed a curling first-time strike past the Arsenal goalkeeper. It was the first away goal the Gunners had conceded in all competitions this campaign. [[Elye Wahi]] completed the turnaround for the hosts in the 69th minute as he fired a first-time finish into the net.<ref name="LEN v ARS UCL SkySports"/><ref name="LEN v ARS UCL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Oct-03/rc-lens-2-1-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: RC Lens 2–1 Arsenal |last=James |first=Josh |date=3 Oct 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=4 Oct 2023 |archive-date=8 Oct 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231008192607/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Oct-03/rc-lens-2-1-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

