Five days later, Arteta's side played at home against 8th-placed [[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton & Hove Albion]]. The Gunners found the breakthrough on 53 minutes when Saka's corner was inadvertently flicked on by Brighton defender [[Jan Paul van Hecke]] for an unmarked Gabriel Jesus to head in at the far post. Havertz sealed the victory for Arsenal in the 87th minute, netting his fourth goal in seven games since returning from international duty. The 2–0 win saw Arsenal extend their unbeaten home run to 13 games in all competitions – their longest run under manager Arteta, and sent them back to the top of the league table, a point above Liverpool.<ref name="ARS v BHA PL BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/67673776 |title=Arsenal 2–0 Brighton & Hove Albion: Gabriel Jesus and Kai Havertz put Gunners top |last=Jurejko |first=Jonathan |date=17 Dec 2023 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=18 Dec 2023 |archive-date=17 Dec 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231217192824/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/67673776 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="ARS v BHA PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Dec-17/arsenal-2-0-brighton-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 2–0 Brighton & Hove Albion |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=17 Dec 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=18 Dec 2023 |archive-date=17 Dec 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231217192218/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Dec-17/arsenal-2-0-brighton-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

The Premier League's top two went head-to-head on 23 December as Arsenal travelled to [[Anfield]] to play against Liverpool. Gabriel Magalhães opened the scoring on four minutes, heading in from Ødegaard's set-piece. Liverpool levelled through [[Mohamed Salah]] on 29 minutes. Both teams had chances to win the game after that, but neither side could find the breakthrough.<ref name="LIV v ARS PL">{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3835037 |title=Arsenal TOP at Christmas after entertaining draw at Liverpool |date=23 Dec 2023 |website=Premier League |access-date=24 Dec 2023 |archive-date=23 Dec 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231223234217/https://www.premierleague.com/news/3835037 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="LIV v ARS PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Dec-23/liverpool-1-1-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Liverpool 1–1 Arsenal |last=James |first=Josh |date=23 Dec 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=24 Dec 2023 |archive-date=23 Dec 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231223214256/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Dec-23/liverpool-1-1-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> The Anfield pitch seemed to have played a factor in the match, as both sets of players slipped over throughout the game.<ref name="LIV v ARS PL NowArsenal">{{cite web |url=https://www.nowarsenal.com/latest-news/how-anfields-slippery-pitch-will-affect-the-unpredictable-title-race/ |title=How Anfield's slippery pitch will affect the unpredictable title race |last=Chambers |first=Lucas |date=24 Dec 2023 |website=nowarsenal.com |access-date=24 Dec 2023 |archive-date=28 Dec 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231228164112/https://www.nowarsenal.com/latest-news/how-anfields-slippery-pitch-will-affect-the-unpredictable-title-race/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="LIV v ARS PL DailyCannon">{{cite web |url=https://dailycannon.com/2023/12/anfield-pitch-liverpool-arsenal/ |title=Liverpool pay the price for awful Anfield pitch against Arsenal |last=Critchlow |first=Dan |date=24 Dec 2023 |website=DailyCannon |access-date=24 Dec 2023 |archive-date=28 Dec 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231228164112/https://dailycannon.com/2023/12/anfield-pitch-liverpool-arsenal/ |url-status=live}}</ref> In the 19th minute, Ødegaard slipped in his own box whilst changing direction, and his hand falling on the ball. The hosts wanted a spot-kick, though a VAR check stayed with the on-field decision not to award a penalty.<ref name="LIV v ARS PL SkySports">{{cite web |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/liverpool-vs-arsenal/report/482764 |title=Liverpool 1–1 Arsenal: Mohamed Salah cancels out Gabriel opener but Mikel Arteta's Gunners top of Premier League at Christmas |last=Blitz |first=Sam |date=23 Dec 2023 |website=Sky Sports |access-date=24 Dec 2023 |archive-date=23 Dec 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231223195732/http://www.skysports.com/football/liverpool-vs-arsenal/report/482764 |url-status=live}}</ref> In the 33rd minute, Saka slipped near the touchline and nudged defender [[Kostas Tsimikas]] into manager [[Jürgen Klopp]], resulting in both Liverpool men falling to the ground. Tsimikas was forced off with an injury, and was revealed to have broken his collarbone in the collision with Klopp.<ref name="LIV v ARS PL"/><ref name="LIV v ARS PL NowArsenal"/> In the 41st minute, Martinelli slipped in the box before firing wide with goalkeeper [[Alisson Becker|Alisson]] off his line.<ref name="LIV v ARS PL SkySports"/><ref name="LIV v ARS PL NowArsenal"/> In the 72nd minute, Zinchenko slipped and went in Ødegaard's way from an Arsenal corner, allowing Liverpool to counter. Salah led a five on two attack, but Alexander-Arnold smashed the crossbar from close range.<ref name="LIV v ARS PL SkySports"/> In the 88th minute, Saka competed for the ball with Liverpool's [[Ryan Gravenberch]]. The Englishman won the ball but slipped and the two collided at midfield.<ref name="LIV v ARS PL DailyCannon"/> The 1–1 draw meant that the Gunners would start Christmas Day top of the English top-flight table in two consecutive years for just a second time, after last doing so in 1932 and 1933.<ref name="LIV v ARS PL"/>

Arsenal suffered their first home defeat of the season with a 2–0 loss to 6th-placed West Ham United on 28 December. In the 13th minute, [[Jarrod Bowen]] pulled the ball back from the byline to [[Tomáš Souček]], who tapped home from close range. While the ball had potentially gone out of play before Bowen's pass, there was no clear angle available for the VAR to determine whether it was in or out, and the goal was awarded. [[Konstantinos Mavropanos]] doubled the lead on 55 minutes, becoming the first former Arsenal player to score his first goal in the Premier League against the Gunners. Rice gave away a penalty in stoppage time, but [[Saïd Benrahma]]'s effort was saved by Raya. Arteta's side reached the halfway point of the league campaign with twelve wins, four draws, three losses and 40 points from 19 games, two points behind leaders Liverpool.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3840746 |title=West Ham win derby to stop Arsenal returning to top spot |date=28 Dec 2023 |website=Premier League |access-date=29 Dec 2023}}</ref><ref name="ARS v WHU PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/report-arsenal-0-2-west-ham-united |title=Report: Arsenal 0–2 West Ham United |last=James |first=Josh |date=28 Dec 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=29 Dec 2023 |archive-date=28 Dec 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231228233842/https://www.arsenal.com/news/report-arsenal-0-2-west-ham-united |url-status=live}}</ref>

The Gunners concluded 2023 with a trip across the capital to face 13th-placed Fulham at [[Craven Cottage]] on New Year's Eve. Saka opened the scoring from close range in the 5th minute. The Cottagers drew level with a counter-attacking goal scored by [[Raúl Jiménez]] on 29 minutes. [[Bobby Decordova-Reid]] completed the turnaround for Fulham in the 59th minute. The 2–1 loss ended Arsenal's run of 48 league games unbeaten when going 1–0 ahead. The result left the Gunners sitting fourth in the Premier League table.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3840360 |title=Jimenez and De Cordova-Reid deny Arsenal top spot |date=31 Dec 2023 |website=Premier League |access-date=1 Jan 2024}}</ref><ref name="FUL v ARS PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Dec-31/fulham-2-1-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Fulham 2–1 Arsenal |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=31 Dec 2023 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=1 Jan 2024 |archive-date=1 Jan 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240101000332/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2023-Dec-31/fulham-2-1-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

===January===
Elneny was named in Egypt's squad for the [[2023 Africa Cup of Nations]], which was held in Ivory Coast from 13 January to 11 February.<ref name="Egypt call-ups Jan 2024 AFC"/> Tomiyasu was called up by Japan for the [[2023 AFC Asian Cup]], which took place in Qatar from 12 January to 10 February.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/tomiyasu-called-japan-asian-cup |title=Tomiyasu called up by Japan for Asian Cup |date=2 Jan 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=2 Jan 2024}}</ref> Elneny's Egypt were knocked out in the round of 16 on penalties on 28 January.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/elneny-out-afcon-egypt |title=Elneny out of AFCON with Egypt |last=Wright |first=Stephen |date=29 Jan 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=29 Jan 2024 |archive-date=29 Jan 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240129104904/https://www.arsenal.com/news/elneny-out-afcon-egypt |url-status=live}}</ref> Tomiyasu's Japan were eliminated in the quarter-finals on 3 February.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/tomiyasu-and-japan-out-asian-cup |title=Tomiyasu and Japan out of the Asian Cup |date=3 Feb 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=3 Feb 2024 |archive-date=3 Feb 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240203185014/https://www.arsenal.com/news/tomiyasu-and-japan-out-asian-cup |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 7 January, Arteta's side entered the [[2023–24 FA Cup|FA Cup]] in the third round, facing Liverpool at home. The Gunners created many goal-scoring opportunities, but failed to convert any. In the 80th minute, Kiwior's own goal handed Liverpool the lead. In the last minute of stoppage time, Ramsdale's long pass was intercepted, the ball eventually went to [[Luis Díaz (footballer, born 1997)|Luis Díaz]], whose goal knocked Arsenal out of the competition.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/67892587 |title=Arsenal 0–2 Liverpool: Jakub Kiwior own goal and Luis Diaz strike in FA Cup win for Reds |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=7 Jan 2024 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=8 Jan 2024 |archive-date=8 Jan 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240108015012/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/67892587 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="ARS v LIV FACup AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Jan-07/arsenal-0-2-liverpool-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 0–2 Liverpool |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=7 Jan 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=8 Jan 2024 |archive-date=24 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240524195750/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Jan-07/arsenal-0-2-liverpool-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 9 January, the Gunners travelled to Dubai, United Arab Emirates for a week-long training camp.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/mens-first-team-head-dubai-training-camp |title=Men's first-team head to Dubai training camp |date=9 Jan 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=10 Jan 2024 |archive-date=9 Jan 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240109145100/https://www.arsenal.com/news/mens-first-team-head-dubai-training-camp |url-status=live}}</ref> They flew back to England on 16 January.

Arteta's men returned to action after the winter break with a game against 14th-placed Crystal Palace at home on 20 January. Gabriel Magalhães nodded in Rice's corner in the 11th minute, and then his header from a Saka corner deflected in off visiting goalkeeper [[Dean Henderson]] on 37 minutes. Arsenal's third goal came in the 59th minute, as Raya claimed a cross following a Palace corner and his 40-yard throw sent Gabriel Jesus on his way; the Brazilian then swept the ball across to Trossard, who beaten the last defender before rifling beyond Henderson. Substitute Martinelli netted twice from counter-attacks in stoppage time, sealing a 5–0 victory for the hosts. The result ended Arsenal's three-match losing run in all competitions, moving them up to third place in the Premier League table.<ref name="ARS v CRY PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Jan-20/arsenal-5-0-crystal-palace-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 5–0 Crystal Palace |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=20 Jan 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=21 Jan 2024 |archive-date=24 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240524195753/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Jan-20/arsenal-5-0-crystal-palace-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5203270/2024/01/20/arsenal-crystal-palace-premier-league-analysis/ |title=Arsenal 5 Crystal Palace 0: Superb set-pieces, Trossard and Martinelli shine |last=de Roché |first=Art |date=21 Jan 2024 |website=The Athletic |access-date=21 Jan 2024 |archive-date=24 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240524195750/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5203270/2024/01/20/arsenal-crystal-palace-premier-league-analysis/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Arsenal headed to the [[City Ground]] to take on 16th-placed Nottingham Forest on 30 January. Gabriel Jesus opened the scoring on 65 minutes as he poked the ball home from a tight angle. Saka doubled the lead on 72 minutes with a right-footed effort, becoming the first player to record 10+ goals and 10+ assists in all competitions in two consecutive seasons for the Gunners since [[2017–18 Arsenal F.C. season|2017–18]]. Forest substitute Taiwo Awoniyi pulled a goal back in the 89th minute.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3871501 |title=Arsenal go two points off the top ahead of duel with Liverpool |date=30 Jan 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=31 Jan 2024}}</ref><ref name="NFO v ARS PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Jan-30/nottingham-forest-1-2-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Nottingham Forest 1–2 Arsenal |date=30 Jan 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=31 Jan 2024 |archive-date=31 Jan 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240131005251/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Jan-30/nottingham-forest-1-2-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

====First-team transfers (winter transfer window)====
The Premier League [[List of English football transfers winter 2023–24|winter transfer window]] ran from 1 January to 1 February 2024. The club announced on 12 January that Brazilian forward Marquinhos had been recalled from his loan at Ligue 1 side Nantes.<ref name="Marquinhos AFC 2"/>

On English transfer deadline day, 1 February, Arsenal confirmed that Icelandic goalkeeper Rúnar Alex Rúnarsson had been recalled from his loan at Championship side Cardiff City and would depart the club following the termination of his contract.<ref name="Rúnarsson AFC 2"/>

On 15 February, the Gunners announced that Marquinhos had joined [[Campeonato Brasileiro Série A|Série A]] side [[Fluminense FC|Fluminense]] on loan until January 2025.<ref name="Marquinhos AFC 3"/>

After the winter transfer window closed, there were twenty-five players in the first-team squad: three goalkeepers, eight defenders, eight midfielders, and six forwards. Five other first-team players were out on loan.<ref name="first-team squad (Feb) AFC"/>

===February===
