Ahead of the last international break of the season, Arteta's side hosted Porto in the second leg of the Champions League round of 16 on 12 March. The Gunners cancelled out Porto's first-leg advantage on 41 minutes when Ødegaard slipped the ball through three defenders to Trossard, who fired a low shot into the far corner.<ref name="ARS v POR UCL BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/68538063 |title=Arsenal 1–0 Porto (Agg 1–1, Arsenal win 4–2 on penalties): Gunners reach Champions League quarter-finals |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=12 Mar 2024 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=14 Mar 2024 |archive-date=14 Mar 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240314161142/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/68538063 |url-status=live}}</ref> The Belgian became the third player to score in each of his first three Champions League home games for Arsenal, after [[Alexis Sánchez]] and Saka.<ref name="ARS v POR UCL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Mar-12/arsenal-1-0-porto-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 1–0 Porto (AET, 4–2 pens) |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=12 Mar 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=14 Mar 2024 |archive-date=13 Mar 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313025250/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Mar-12/arsenal-1-0-porto-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> There were no further goals in the second half and [[extra-time]]. In the penalty shootout, Ødegaard, Havertz, Saka and Rice were all on target for the Gunners.<ref name="ARS v POR UCL BBC"/> Raya made two saves out of the four penalties he faced, becoming the first Arsenal goalkeeper to make two stops in a shootout since [[Bernd Leno]] against Liverpool in 2020.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/arsenal-spot-our-penalty-shootout-history |title=Arsenal on the spot: Our penalty shootout history |last=James |first=Josh |date=13 Mar 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=13 Mar 2024 |archive-date=13 Mar 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313173913/https://www.arsenal.com/news/arsenal-spot-our-penalty-shootout-history |url-status=live}}</ref> The Gunners progressed to the quarter-final of the Champions League for the first time since [[2009–10 Arsenal F.C. season|2009–10]], ending a run of seven consecutive last-16 eliminations in the competition.<ref name="ARS v POR UCL AFC"/>

On 14 March, the club announced that English defender Ben White had signed a new long-term contract.<ref name="White new contract AFC"/><ref name="White new contract BBC"/> Six day later, Arsenal confirmed that Japanese defender Takehiro Tomiyasu had penned a new long-term contract.<ref name="Tomiyasu new contract AFC"/><ref name="Tomiyasu new contract BBC"/>

Thirteen Arsenal first-team players (excluding players who were loaned out) were named in their respective countries' senior squads for international fixtures in March: Trossard (Belgium), Gabriel Magalhães (Brazil), Rice (as England's captain),<ref name="Rice & Hein AFC"/> Ramsdale and Saka (England), Hein (as Estonia's captain),<ref name="Rice & Hein AFC"/> Saliba (France), Havertz (Germany), Jorginho (Italy), Ødegaard (as Norway's captain), Kiwior (Poland), Raya (Spain), and Zinchenko (as Ukraine's captain).<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/where-and-when-our-13-internationals-are-action |title=Where and when our 13 internationals are in action |last=Wright |first=Stephen |date=18 Mar 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=18 Mar 2024}}</ref>

Following the international break, the Gunners faced 3rd-placed Manchester City at [[City of Manchester Stadium|Etihad Stadium]] on 31 March. Neither side was able to break the deadlock and the game finished 0–0.<ref name="MCI v ARS PL">{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3943544 |title=Arsenal frustrate Man City as goalless draw leaves Liverpool top |date=31 Mar 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=1 Apr 2024}}</ref> The result ended Arsenal's eight-match losing streak in all competitions at the Etihad,<ref name="MCI v ARS PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Mar-31/manchester-city-0-0-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Manchester City 0–0 Arsenal |last=James |first=Josh |date=31 Mar 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=1 Apr 2024 |archive-date=1 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240401081020/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Mar-31/manchester-city-0-0-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> making Arteta the first manager to go unbeaten against both Jürgen Klopp and Pep Guardiola in a Premier League season.<ref>{{cite tweet |author=Football on TNT Sports |user=footballontnt |number=1774879596002824523 |date=1 Apr 2024 |title=Only one manager has gone unbeaten against both Jurgen Klopp and Pep Guardiola in a Premier League season. Mikel Arteta 🇪🇸 |access-date=1 Apr 2024}}</ref> The draw meant that the Gunners dropped to second in the league, two points behind Liverpool and a point above Manchester City.<ref name="MCI v ARS PL"/>

===April===
Arsenal hosted 18th-placed Luton Town on 3 April for the first of eight fixtures in the month. In the 24th minute Havertz played a pass to Ødegaard, who slammed a first-time finish beyond visiting goalkeeper. An own goal from Hatters defender [[Daiki Hashioka]] on 44 minutes ensured that the Gunners claimed a 2–0 win.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3944247 |title=Arsenal see off Luton to return to top spot |date=3 Apr 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=4 Apr 2024}}</ref><ref name="ARS v LUT PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-03/arsenal-2-0-luton-town-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 2–0 Luton Town |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=3 Apr 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=4 Apr 2024 |archive-date=4 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240404000747/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-03/arsenal-2-0-luton-town-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 6 April, Arteta's side travelled Southwards to the [[Falmer Stadium|Amex Stadium]] to face 9th-place Brighton & Hove Albion. Saka opened the scoring from the spot after [[Tariq Lamptey]] tripped Gabriel Jesus. Havertz finished from Jorginho's cutback in the second half, before Trossard added a late third with a chipped goal. The 3–0 win meant that the Gunners had kept five consecutive clean sheets away from home in the Premier League for the first time since 1997,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3952634 |title=Arsenal ease past Brighton to reclaim top spot |date=6 Apr 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=7 Apr 2024}}</ref><ref name="BHA v ARS PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-06/brighton-0-3-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Brighton & Hove Albion 0–3 Arsenal |last=James |first=Josh |date=6 Apr 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=7 Apr 2024 |archive-date=6 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240406230711/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-06/brighton-0-3-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> and Raya became the first Spanish goalkeeper to keep five clean sheets in as many away starts in the competition.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5393751/2024/04/06/brighton-0-arsenal-3-saka-havertz-title/ |title=Brighton 0 Arsenal 3: Clinical Saka, devilish Havertz and a title charge in rude health |last=de Roché |first=Art |date=6 Apr 2024 |website=The Athletic |access-date=7 Apr 2024 |archive-date=7 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240407002725/https://theathletic.com/5393751/2024/04/06/brighton-0-arsenal-3-saka-havertz-title/ |url-status=live}}</ref> The result put Arsenal back on top of the table, before Manchester United managed to hold Liverpool to a draw on the following day.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3958124 |title=Advantage Arsenal as Man Utd hold Liverpool to 2–2 draw |date=7 Apr 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=7 Apr 2024 |archive-date=24 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240524195751/https://www.premierleague.com/news/3958124 |url-status=live}}</ref>

The Gunners hosted German giant Bayern Munich in the Champions League quarter-final first leg on 9 April. Saka gave Arsenal an early advantage; but the six-time European champions drew level thanks to former Gunner [[Serge Gnabry]], and then went ahead through a [[Harry Kane]] penalty. Substitute Trossard netted an equaliser in the second half, becoming the fourth player in Champions League history to score in each of his first four home appearances in the competition.<ref name="ARS v BAY UCL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-09/arsenal-2-2-bayern-munich-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 2–2 Bayern Munich |last=James |first=Josh |date=9 Apr 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=10 Apr 2024 |archive-date=9 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240409232459/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-09/arsenal-2-2-bayern-munich-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> The match ended 2–2.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/028c-1a9d3cf22106-146814ceec63-1000--arsenal-2-2-bayern-honours-even-heading-to-munich-after-thri/ |title=Arsenal 2–2 Bayern: Honours even heading to Munich after thrilling first leg |date=9 Apr 2024 |website=[[UEFA]] |access-date=10 Apr 2024 |archive-date=20 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240420160907/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/028c-1a9d3cf22106-146814ceec63-1000--arsenal-2-2-bayern-honours-even-heading-to-munich-after-thri/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Arsenal played at home against 5th-placed Aston Villa on 14 April. Arteta's men dominated the first 45 minutes and had plenty of chances to score, but failed to convert any. They struggled to create many opportunities in the second half. Late goals from Villa forwards [[Leon Bailey]] and [[Ollie Watkins]] saw Arsenal's 11-league game unbeaten run come to an end.<ref name="ARS v AVL PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-14/arsenal-0-2-aston-villa-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 0–2 Aston Villa |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=14 Apr 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=15 Apr 2024 |archive-date=14 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240414215353/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-14/arsenal-0-2-aston-villa-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> The 0–2 defeat left the Gunners in second – two points behind Manchester City.<ref name="ARS v AVL PL">{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3967327 |title=Arsenal miss chance to go top as late goals boost Villa's top-four hopes |date=14 Apr 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=15 Apr 2024 |archive-date=15 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240415100000/https://www.premierleague.com/news/3967327 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 17 April, Arteta's side travelled to Bayern Munich for the second leg of their Champions League quarter-final. [[Joshua Kimmich]] scored the only goal of the tight game with a header in the 63rd minute, helping the hosts win 3–2 on aggregate. The result meant that Arsenal's first Champions League campaign in seven years ended in the last eight.<ref name="BAY v ARS UCL UEFA">{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/028c-1aad55280ea2-7e79cc7df88b-1000--bayern-1-0-arsenal-agg-3-2-kimmich-header-takes-home-side-th/ |title=Bayern 1–0 Arsenal (agg: 3–2): Kimmich header takes home side through |date=17 Apr 2024 |website=UEFA |access-date=18 Apr 2024 |archive-date=20 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240420160907/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/028c-1aad55280ea2-7e79cc7df88b-1000--bayern-1-0-arsenal-agg-3-2-kimmich-header-takes-home-side-th/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="BAY v ARS UCL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-17/bayern-munich-1-0-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Bayern Munich 1–0 Arsenal |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=17 Apr 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=18 Apr 2024 |archive-date=17 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240417213927/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-17/bayern-munich-1-0-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

Arsenal headed to [[Molineux Stadium]] to take on 11th-placed Wolverhampton Wanderers on 20 April. Goals from Trossard and Ødegaard late in each half sealed a 2–0 victory for the visitors. This was the first time Arsenal had kept a clean sheet in six consecutive Premier League away games.<ref name="WOL v ARS PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-20/wolves-0-2-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Wolves 0–2 Arsenal |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=20 Apr 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=21 Apr 2024 |archive-date=21 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240421013827/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-20/wolves-0-2-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> Raya became the second goalkeeper in Premier League history to keep six straight away clean sheets.<ref name="WOL v ARS PL">{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3971799 |title=Arsenal win at Wolves to return to TOP spot |date=20 Apr 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=21 Apr 2024 |archive-date=23 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240423144850/https://www.premierleague.com/news/3971799 |url-status=live}}</ref> The win sent the Gunners back to the top of the league table, a point above Manchester City who had a game in hand.<ref name="WOL v ARS PL"/>

