}}
On 23 April, Arteta's side played the postponed match at home to 9th-placed Chelsea. Trossard gave Arsenal an early lead with their 100th goal of the season in all competitions.<ref name="ARS v CHE PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-23/arsenal-5-0-chelsea-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 5–0 Chelsea |last=Wright |first=Stephen |date=23 Apr 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=24 Apr 2024 |archive-date=24 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240424011925/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-23/arsenal-5-0-chelsea-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> A dominant second-half display saw Havertz and White both score twice.<ref name="ARS v CHE PL BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/68519746 |title=Arsenal 5–0 Chelsea: Kai Havertz scores twice against former side as Gunners go three points clear |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=23 Apr 2024 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=24 Apr 2024 |archive-date=23 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240423233517/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/68519746 |url-status=live}}</ref> The 5–0 win meant that the Gunners recorded their biggest ever victory against Chelsea across all competitions, becoming the first team in English league history to win three London derbies by five or more goals in a single campaign.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3977136 |title=Arsenal thrash Chelsea to go three points clear at the top |date=23 Apr 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=24 Apr 2024 |archive-date=24 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240424024211/https://www.premierleague.com/news/3977136 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 28 April, Arsenal faced 5th-placed Tottenham at [[Tottenham Hotspur Stadium]] in the second North London derby of the season. Arteta's side raced into a 3–0 half-time lead through a [[Pierre-Emile Højbjerg]] own goal, a Saka finish and a Havertz header. The hosts pulled two back in the second half, as Cristian Romero punished Raya's error and Son Heung-min scored from the spot after Rice fouled [[Ben Davies (footballer, born 1993)|Ben Davies]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3985965 |title=Arsenal win FIVE-goal north London derby thriller |date=28 Apr 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=29 Apr 2024 |archive-date=29 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240429005037/https://www.premierleague.com/news/3985965 |url-status=live}}</ref> The 3–2 victory meant that the Gunners had won consecutive top-flight away games against Tottenham for the first time since 1988.<ref name="TOT v ARS PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-28/tottenham-hotspur-2-3-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Tottenham Hotspur 2–3 Arsenal |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=28 Apr 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=29 Apr 2024 |archive-date=28 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240428211836/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-Apr-28/tottenham-hotspur-2-3-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> This was Arteta's 100th Premier League win as Arsenal manager, from his 169th game in charge.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/mikel-arteta-reaches-100-premier-league-wins |title=Mikel Arteta reaches 100 Premier League wins |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=29 Apr 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=29 Apr 2024 |archive-date=29 Apr 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240429143842/https://www.arsenal.com/news/mikel-arteta-reaches-100-premier-league-wins |url-status=live}}</ref>

===May===
The club started May with a 3–0 win over 10th-placed Bournemouth at home on 4 May. Saka opened the scoring from the penalty spot before the break, becoming the first player to score 20 goals for Arsenal in a season in all competitions since [[Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang]] in [[2019–20 Arsenal F.C. season|2019–20]] and the first Englishman to do so for the club since Theo Walcott in [[2012–13 Arsenal F.C. season|2012–13]].<ref name="ARS v BOU PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-May-04/arsenal-3-0-bournemouth-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 3–0 Bournemouth |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=4 May 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=5 May 2024 |archive-date=4 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240504211850/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-May-04/arsenal-3-0-bournemouth-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref> Trossard and Rice both found the net in the second half.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/3991651 |title=Arsenal beat Bournemouth to keep hold of top spot |date=4 May 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=5 May 2024 |archive-date=5 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240505075909/https://www.premierleague.com/news/3991651 |url-status=live}}</ref> The result ensured Raya kept his 15th Premier League clean sheet of the season, a total that could not be matched. He became the third Arsenal goalkeeper to win the [[Premier League Golden Glove]] since its inception during the [[2004–05 Premier League|2004–05]] season, after [[Wojciech Szczęsny]] and [[Petr Čech]]. Raya was the third Spaniard to claim the award after [[Pepe Reina]] and [[David de Gea]].<ref name="Raya won PL Golden Glove"/><ref name="Raya won PL Golden Glove AFC"/>

On 9 May, Arsenal announced that Italian midfielder Jorginho had signed a new contract.<ref name="Jorginho new contract AFC"/><ref name="Jorginho new contract TheAthletic"/>

In their final away game of the season, Arteta's side faced 8th-placed Manchester United at [[Old Trafford]] on 12 May. Trossard scored the only goal to clinch victory for the visitors. This was the first time Arsenal had completed the league double over Manchester United since 2006–07.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/4002047 |title=Arsenal take title race to final day with win at Man Utd |date=12 May 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=14 May 2024 |archive-date=13 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240513104030/https://www.premierleague.com/news/4002047 |url-status=live}}</ref> It was also the first time since 1998 that the Gunners had won three successive Premier League games against the Red Devils.<ref name="MUN v ARS PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-May-12/manchester-united-0-1-arsenal-match-report |title=Report: Manchester United 0–1 Arsenal |last=James |first=Josh |date=12 May 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=14 May 2024 |archive-date=12 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240512225351/https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-May-12/manchester-united-0-1-arsenal-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

Arsenal's 52nd and final game of the campaign was at home against 15th-placed Everton on 19 May. The visitors took the lead on 40 minutes when [[Idrissa Gueye]]'s free-kick deflected off Rice in the defensive wall and flew into the far corner. Tomiyasu levelled for the Gunners in the 43th minute. Timber made his first-team return as a 69th-minute replacement for White, marking a seven-month wait for his second Premier League appearance after his ACL injury on the opening day of the season. Havertz scored an 89th-minute goal from close range, helping his side win 2–1. The result meant that Arteta's side finished their 2023–24 Premier League campaign in second place, two points behind champions Manchester City.<ref name="ARS v EVE PL">{{cite web |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/4016169 |title=Arsenal fall short despite late win over Everton |date=19 May 2024 |website=Premier League |access-date=20 May 2024}}</ref><ref name="ARS v EVE PL AFC">{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/fixture/arsenal/2024-May-19/arsenal-2-1-everton-match-report |title=Report: Arsenal 2–1 Everton |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=19 May 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=20 May 2024}}</ref>

===June===
The Gunners' [[UEFA coefficient#Men's club coefficient|UEFA club coefficient]] was 72.000 points at the end of this campaign.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/nationalassociations/uefarankings/club/?year=2024 |title=Club coefficients {{pipe}} UEFA rankings |website=UEFA |access-date=3 Jun 2024 |archive-date=3 Jun 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240603161217/https://www.uefa.com/nationalassociations/uefarankings/club/?year=2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> They would be in Pot 2 for the [[2024–25 UEFA Champions League league phase|2024–25 Champions League league phase]] draw.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/football/arsenal-fc-champions-league-projected-pots-liverpool-man-city-b1160415.html |title=Projected Champions League pots revealed for new format as Arsenal miss out but Liverpool join Man City |last=Verri |first=Matt |date=27 May 2024 |website=Evening Standard |access-date=27 May 2024}}</ref>

The [[UEFA Euro 2024]] took place in Germany from 14 June to 14 July. Ten Arsenal players (excluding players who were loaned out) were named in [[UEFA Euro 2024 squads|squads]] for the tournament: Trossard (Belgium), Ramsdale, Rice and Saka (England), Saliba (France), Havertz (Germany), Jorginho (Italy), Kiwior (Poland), Raya (Spain), and Zinchenko (Ukraine).<ref name="Euro 2024 call-ups AFC"/> This was the first time the Gunners had at least ten players selected for a European Championship.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/euro-2024-arsenal-related-guide-finals |title=Euro 2024: An Arsenal-related guide to the finals |last=Rogers |first=Jonathon |date=14 Jun 2024 |website=Arsenal FC |access-date=14 Jun 2024}}</ref>

Gabriel Magalhães and Martinelli were named in Brazil's squad for the [[2024 Copa América]], which was held in the United States from 20 June to 14 July.<ref name="Intl call-ups Jun 2024 AFC"/>

Four other Arsenal first-team players were called up to their respective countries' senior squads for international fixtures in June: Hein (Estonia), Partey (as Ghana's captain),<ref name="Partey as Ghana's captain 2"/> Tomiyasu (Japan), and Ødegaard (as Norway's captain).<ref name="Intl call-ups Jun 2024 AFC"/>
